@page "/blogs/getting-started-with-aspire-in-dotnet"
@using SharedModels
@using BaseComponents
@inherits BasePage

<Content FileName=@nameof(GettingStartedWithAspireInDotNet)>
    <ContentBody>
        <What>
            <p>
                <Highlight>Aspire</Highlight> starts an existing distributed system through one code-defined
                <Highlight>AppHost</Highlight>, which describes its services, databases, tools, and connections.
            </p>
            <p>
                This article covers adding Aspire to an existing application, using
                <Highlight>ServiceDefaults</Highlight>, the dashboard and database resources, and isolated worktree runs. It
                focuses on wiring; services keep their existing business logic.
            </p>
        </What>

        <Why>
            <p>
                Starting many services by hand means managing container runtimes, environment settings, startup order, and
                terminals. A local graph may contain around <Highlight>30 resources</Highlight>, including projects, databases,
                brokers, user interfaces, and tools. This describes the development graph, not 30 containers or a production
                rollout.
            </p>
            <p>
                Recreating the graph for each teammate slows onboarding and creates inconsistent setups. A shared AppHost gives
                developers and AI coding agents an end-to-end view, replacing the startup checklist with one launch and dashboard.
            </p>
            <p>
                Stale data can make a clean checkout behave differently from a long-lived workstation. A fresh database resource
                and the application's migration and seed process provide a predictable baseline; Aspire does not reset or seed
                business data. Use disposable local resources for tests: Aspire does not prevent an AppHost from connecting to
                shared staging data.
            </p>
        </Why>

        <How>
            <h4>1. Initialize Aspire in the solution you already have</h4>
            <p>
                Use the solution's required .NET SDK, a running container runtime, and the Aspire CLI. Install the CLI as a .NET
                global tool if needed:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-powershell">
dotnet tool install -g Aspire.Cli
aspire --version
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Verify the installation with the version command, then open the solution and follow its local configuration
                process. Keep credentials in the team's approved local secret store, not source files.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-powershell">
aspire init
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Let the CLI discover the workspace and follow its prompts; choose C# if asked for the AppHost language. In a
                <Highlight>.sln</Highlight> or <Highlight>.slnx</Highlight> workspace, <Highlight>aspire init</Highlight>
                scaffolds a project-based AppHost with project references and strongly typed
                <Highlight>AddProject</Highlight> calls. It does not infer every application-specific resource, dependency, or
                service setting.
            </p>
            <p>
                Add PostgreSQL hosting support to the AppHost with the CLI; this does not create application data:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-powershell">
aspire add postgres
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                If offered, enable agent setup and ask your coding agent to run the installed
                <Highlight>aspireify</Highlight> skill. It can inspect the solution, propose resources for your review, wire
                dependencies, configure ServiceDefaults and telemetry, and smoke-test with
                <Highlight>aspire start</Highlight>. Review the graph and settings; initialization cannot infer every application
                contract.
            </p>

            <h4>2. Describe the existing production topology in the AppHost</h4>
            <p>
                The AppHost is a separate development-time process, not a business service. This production-shaped example
                anonymizes project and resource names:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
var builder = DistributedApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

var postgres = builder.AddPostgres("postgres")
    .WithImage("pgvector/pgvector", "pg16")
    .WithPgAdmin();

var identityDb = postgres.AddDatabase("identity-db");
var appDb = postgres.AddDatabase("application-db");

var identity = builder.AddProject&lt;Projects.IdentityService&gt;("identity")
    .WithReference(identityDb, "IdentityConnectionString")
    .WaitFor(identityDb);

var api = builder.AddProject&lt;Projects.Api&gt;("api")
    .WithReference(identityDb, "IdentityConnectionString")
    .WithReference(appDb, "CommandsConnectionString")
    .WithReference(appDb, "QueriesConnectionString")
    .WithReference(identity)
    .WaitFor(identity)
    .WaitFor(appDb);

var web = builder.AddProject&lt;Projects.Web&gt;("web")
    .WithHttpHealthCheck("/health")
    .WithReference(api)
    .WithReference(identity)
    .WaitFor(api)
    .WaitFor(identity);

builder.AddProject&lt;Projects.Landing&gt;("landing")
    .WithHttpHealthCheck("/health")
    .WaitFor(web);

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                <Highlight>WithReference</Highlight> supplies service-discovery endpoints or a
                database connection string under the name the service expects.
                <Highlight>WaitFor</Highlight> declares startup dependencies and waits for a
                configured health check, or otherwise for the dependency to run.
                Both <Highlight>web</Highlight> and <Highlight>landing</Highlight> must expose
                <Highlight>/health</Highlight> in the test environment, using the ServiceDefaults
                setup below. This lets the companion fixture wait for HTTP readiness rather than just running processes.
            </p>
            <p>
                The AppHost maps the existing Identity, Commands, and Queries connection-string names to two databases on one
                PostgreSQL server resource. It derives OpenID Connect redirect and logout callbacks from the web HTTPS endpoint
                at runtime instead of hard-coding a machine port. Apps in C#, TypeScript, Python, Go, and other languages can
                consume Aspire's PostgreSQL connection properties with their own database clients.
            </p>

            <h4>3. Use ServiceDefaults for shared service configuration</h4>
            <p>
                <Highlight>ServiceDefaults</Highlight> is shared configuration used by participating
                application projects; the AppHost does not replace it. Aspire templates use it for OpenTelemetry logging,
                metrics and distributed traces, runtime and HTTP instrumentation, service discovery, and shared
                <Highlight>HttpClient</Highlight> defaults. Its generated extension also provides conventions for
                <Highlight>/health</Highlight> and <Highlight>/alive</Highlight>.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
builder.AddServiceDefaults();

var app = builder.Build();
app.MapDefaultEndpoints();
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Keep shared behavior consistent, then customize it centrally. Services need not use identical health checks or
                retries; adjust registrations to meet reliability and readiness requirements. The production-shaped setup
                customizes standard health and resilience registrations. The snippet shows common integration points, not every
                active template default.
            </p>

            <h4>4. Add databases, brokers, and non-.NET resources as needed</h4>
            <p>
                Add integrations such as PostgreSQL or Kafka with the CLI, then model each resource and reference it from its
                consumers. The graph can include non-.NET services, existing images, and administration tools. A broker
                reference supplies configuration and startup coordination; application code still owns migrations, seed data,
                message handling, and business behavior.
            </p>
            <p>
                For a clean local run, use a session-lifetime database container without a persistent volume. The application's
                startup or migration process should create the schema and seed development data before the service reports ready.
                A persistent volume or remote database retains its state; Aspire does not clean it or guarantee isolation from
                shared environments.
            </p>

            <h4>5. Launch once, inspect the graph, and isolate worktrees</h4>
            <p>
                From the AppHost project directory, start the application with:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-powershell">
dotnet run
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                The AppHost starts the modeled resources. Open the dashboard URL in its output to inspect endpoints, health, logs,
                and traces. In an IDE, select the AppHost as the startup project for a one-click launch. Press Ctrl+C to stop the
                session and its managed resources; this is local orchestration, not a production deployment.
            </p>
            <p>
                To run an AI coding agent's application alongside yours in another Git worktree, run
                <Highlight>aspire run --isolated</Highlight> there. Aspire randomizes dashboard and service endpoint ports,
                including configured predictable ports, and isolates instance user secrets. Hard-coded URLs, shared volumes or
                container names, and remote databases remain shared resources; configure them separately when worktrees must not
                affect each other.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-powershell">
aspire run --isolated
            </CodeSnippet>

